    "### What is a N-Gram Model?\n",
    "\n",
    "The N-gram model was not invented for modern AI, nor does it come from Neural Networks.\n",
    "* **The Root**: It is based on Markov Chains, a mathematical concept introduced by Russian mathematician Andrey Markov in 1913. To prove his mathematical theory, Markov manually counted the frequencies of vowels and consonants in Aleksandr Pushkin’s poem Eugene Onegin.\n",
    "* **The Application**: In 1948, Claude Shannon (the father of Information Theory) used this exact method to approximate English text. He showed that by simply tracking which letters or words follow each other, a machine could generate text that looks surprisingly human.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "An N-gram is simply a sequence of \\(N\\) consecutive items (words or characters) from a given slice of text.The \"\\(N\\)\" stands for the number of items in the window. An N-gram model is a classical, statistical/probabilistic model. It does not use neural networks, it does not use weights, and it does not use backpropagation. It is just a massive look-up table of counts\n",
    "\n",
    "Here is how a single sentence—\n",
    "\"I love learning AI\"—is broken down into different N-grams:\n",
    "* Unigram (\\(N=1\\)): Looks at words completely in isolation.[\"I\"], [\"love\"], [\"learning\"], [\"AI\"]\n",
    "* Bigram (\\(N=2\\)): Looks at pairs of words.[\"I\", \"love\"], [\"love\", \"learning\"], [\"learning\", \"AI\"]\n",
    "* Trigram (\\(N=3\\)): Looks at triplets of words.[\"I\", \"love\", \"learning\"], [\"love\", \"learning\", \"AI\"]"
   ]
