    "**What does \"Channel\" (C) mean here?**\n",
    "\n",
    "In our first Bigram model, C happened to be exactly equal to your vocab_size (e.g., 65 unique characters). This was because the row in the table directly held the raw logits (votes) for the next character.In a Transformer, C shifts its meaning to become the \"Embedding Dimension\" (or n_embd).Instead of a row storing raw votes for characters, the row stores a dense coordinate vector (like a point in space) that describes the meaning of that character.say C = 2 in this toy example? This is done here purely for easy print out, visualization, and calculation of the math by hand.Every single character is being represented by a list of just 2 decimal numbers (a 2D coordinate on a graph).In a production model like GPT-3, C is much larger (e.g., 12288), meaning every word is represented by a highly detailed list of 12,288 tracking numbers.3. Why are we doing this toy example step?\n",
    "\n",
    "**We are doing this step to solve a massive structural problem:**\n",
    "\n",
    "The Bigram model was blind to history. It only looked at the very last character to make a prediction.Now, we have expanded our context window say (T = 12). The model can see up to 12 characters at once. But how do we write code that forces those 12 characters to talk to each other and share information?Before jumping into complex Transformers, we write the **simplest mathematical way** to blend the past text data together: Taking a **rolling average of the history**. *(inspired from Karpathy)*"
   ]
