    "## Evolution By far from [Simple BigramLang Model -> BagOfWords -> Self-Attention]\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. The Averaging Approach (Bag of Words)\n",
    "When we started by averaging past tokens (44:13), we were performing a **\"bag of words\" aggregation**. For any given position in the sequence, we calculated the mean of the feature vectors of all preceding tokens (and the token itself).\n",
    "*   **Why average?** It was the simplest way to allow tokens to \"communicate.\" Without this, every token was an island, making predictions based solely on its own identity (24:02). Averaging provides the model with a basic summary of the preceding context.\n",
    "*   **How does it capture semantics?** It barely captures semantics. It is a very lossy, blunt instrument. While it allows information to flow, it treats all past tokens with equal importance—averaging a critical noun is treated the same as averaging a filler word. It completely destroys the spatial arrangement and individual nuance of the context (44:30).\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Adding Flexibility with Linear Transformations\n",
    "By adding linear layers to produce keys, queries, and values, we moved away from static, uniform averaging. \n",
    "*   **The limitation of the simple average:** In the basic average, the weights were essentially fixed (or uniform). \n",
    "*   **Why move to Self-Attention?** Even with linear layers, we need a way to make the communication **data-dependent**. Simple averaging says, \"Give me the average of everything that came before.\" Self-attention says, \"I am looking for specific information (the **Query**), and different tokens in my past have different things to offer (the **Key**).\" \n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. Why Self-Attention is superior\n",
    "Self-attention solves the problem of \"how much of each past token should I pay attention to?\" rather than just averaging them all:\n",
    "*   **Data-Dependent Affinities:** Instead of a uniform average, we calculate the dot product between the **Query** (what I am looking for) and the **Key** (what you contain). This results in an affinity score (106:04).\n",
    "*   **Selective Communication:** By passing these affinities through a `softmax` (109:47), we create a distribution that highlights the most \"interesting\" tokens in the history while ignoring irrelevant ones. \n",
    "*   **Dynamic Aggregation:** We don't just aggregate the raw features; we aggregate the **Values** (what the token actually communicates to others). This allows the network to dynamically focus on specific relationships, such as a verb looking for its subject, rather than just taking a mindless, global average.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Mechanism**: Each token at every position produces three vectors via linear projections: a Query (), a Key (), and a Value ().\n",
    "* **Affinity Scores**: The head computes the dot product between the Query of a token and the Keys of other tokens. This results in an affinity matrix (or weights), which determines how much interest tokens have in each other.\n",
    "* **Data-Dependent Communication**: By applying a softmax to these affinity scores, the head creates a probability distribution that highlights the most relevant tokens in the past. This enables data-dependent aggregation, where tokens can focus on semantically important information rather than just averaging everything equally.\n",
    "* **Value Aggregation**: Finally, the head outputs a weighted sum of the Values () based on the computed affinities. This effectively summarizes the context for each position.\n",
    "  \n",
    "In short, a single head is one dedicated pathway for a token to gather specific, relevant information from its history, setting the stage for multi-headed attention, where multiple such heads operate in parallel to capture different types of relationships simultaneously. In essence, self-attention transforms the model from a \"dumb\" summarizer into a dynamic system that can selectively extract the most relevant semantic information from the past to make an informed prediction about the future."
   ]
