   "source": [
    "Few minor changes we did later are:\n",
    "\n",
    "* We have drafted the slef attemntion mechanism of communication - the tokens can now cummunicate with each other and non-uniformly combine too\n",
    "* The scaling operation—dividing the dot product of the Queries ($Q$) and Keys ($K$) by $\\sqrt{head\\_size}$ (often denoted as $d_k$ in research papers)—is a critical step to ensure stable training at initialization (1:17:16). The logic behind it:\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. Controlling Variance:**\n",
    "* Assume our $Q$ and $K$ vectors are initialized with values that follow a standard distribution (e.g., zero mean and unit variance). \n",
    "* When we compute the dot product of two such vectors of length $d_k$, the resulting sum involves $d_k$ terms. The variance of the resulting sum grows linearly with $d_k$. \n",
    "* Specifically, if each element has unit variance, the dot product will have a variance equal to $d_k$ .\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. Preventing the \"Softmax Saturation\" Problem:**\n",
    "* The dot product results are passed through a `softmax` function. `softmax` is sensitive to the magnitude of the inputs. \n",
    "* If the variance of the dot product is high (i.e., the values are very large or very small), the `softmax` outputs become extremely \"peaky.\" This means one value approaches **1.0** while the others approach **0.0**.\n",
    "* If the output is too peaky, the gradients during backpropagation will become vanishingly small (essentially zero). This prevents the model from learning effectively at the start of training because the neurons don't get meaningful signals to update their weights.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. Restoring Stability:**\n",
    "* By dividing by $\\sqrt{d_k}$, you scale the dot product down so that its variance is returned to **1**. \n",
    "* This ensures that the input to the `softmax` is not excessively large or extreme, keeping the probability distribution \"diffuse\" (more uniform) at initialization. This allows the model to pay attention to multiple tokens across the context window rather than prematurely locking onto a single one.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Earlier we have build the key, query and value vectors - now we divide the result of the dot product by the square root of the dimension of the key vectors, to scale down the numbers so training stays stable\n",
    "\n",
    "This way the mechanism calculates the relationship between words using Queries (Q), Keys (K), and Values (V)"
   ]
